00:15:32,979 --> 00:15:39,040
“Look there!” and then sought admiration from my eyes.

214
00:15:39,040 --> 00:15:41,619
He had shown it so often that I think it

215
00:15:41,619 --> 00:15:44,797
was more real to him now than the house itself.

216
00:15:44,797 --> 00:15:46,540
“Jimmy sent it to me.

217
00:15:46,540 --> 00:15:51,279
I think it’s a very pretty picture. It shows up well.” “Very well.

218
00:15:51,279 --> 00:15:55,360
Had you seen him lately you come out to see me two years ago and

219
00:15:55,360 --> 00:15:58,119
and bought me the house I live in now. Of

220
00:15:58,119 --> 00:16:02,740
course we was broke up when you run off from home, but I see now there was a

221
00:16:02,740 --> 00:16:07,000
reason for it. He knew he had a big future in front of him. And ever since he

222
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:13,060
made a success he was very generous with me.” He seemed reluctant to put away the picture,

223
00:16:13,060 --> 00:16:16,840
held it for another minute, lingeringly, before my eyes.

224
00:16:16,840 --> 00:16:21,040
Then he returned the wallet and pulled from his pocket a ragged old copy of a

225
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:26,560
book called Hopalong Cassidy “Look here, this is a book he had when he

226
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:32,137
was a boy. It just shows you.” He opened it at the back cover and

227
00:16:32,137 --> 00:16:34,297
turned it around for me to see.

228
00:16:34,297 --> 00:16:41,319
On the last flyleaf was printed the word schedule and the date September 12, 1906..

229
00:16:41,319 --> 00:16:48,637
And underneath: Rise from bed six o'clock AM dumbbell exercise and wall scaling 6: :15

230
00:16:48,637 --> 00:16:53,619
to 6: :30. Study electricity, etc. 7: :15 to 8:

231
00:16:53,619 --> 00:17:02,378
:15. Work 8: :30 to 4: :30 PM Baseball and sports 4: :30 to 5. practice elocution,

232
00:17:02,378 --> 00:17:09,457
poise and how to retain it five to six Study needed inventions seven to nine

233
00:17:09,457 --> 00:17:16,240
General Resolves No wasting time at Shafters or [a name, indecipherable] No

234
00:17:16,240 --> 00:17:22,000
more smoking or chewing. Bath every other day Read one improving Booker magazine

235
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:31,957
per week Save five dollars crossed out three dollars per week Be better to parents

236
00:17:31,957 --> 00:17:38,137
“I came across this book by accident,” said the old man. “It just shows you, don’t it?”

237
00:17:38,137 --> 00:17:44,080
“It just shows you.” “Jimmy was bound to get ahead. He always had some resolves like

238
00:17:44,080 --> 00:17:45,340
this or something.

239
00:17:45,340 --> 00:17:48,520
Do you notice what he’s got about improving his mind?

240
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:50,560
He was always great for that.

241
00:17:50,560 --> 00:17:54,637
He told me I ate like a hog once, and I beat him for it.”

242
00:17:54,637 --> 00:18:01,419
He was reluctant to close the book, reading each item aloud and then looking eagerly at me.

243
00:18:01,419 --> 00:18:06,759
I think he rather expected me to copy down the list for my own use.

244
00:18:06,759 --> 00:18:12,520
A little before three the Lutheran minister arrived from Flushing mm-hmm

245
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:17,619
and I began to look involuntarily out the windows for other cars.

246
00:18:17,619 --> 00:18:19,779
So did Gatsby’s father.

247
00:18:19,779 --> 00:18:23,860
And as the time passed and the servants came in and stood waiting in the hall,

248
00:18:23,860 --> 00:18:31,297
his eyes began to blink anxiously, and he spoke of the rain in a worried, uncertain way.

249
00:18:31,297 --> 00:18:36,637
The minister glanced several times at his watch, so I took him aside and asked him to

250
00:18:36,637 --> 00:18:38,919
wait for a half an hour.

251
00:18:38,919 --> 00:18:41,137
But it wasn’t any use.

252
00:18:41,137 --> 00:18:42,878
Nobody came.

253
00:18:42,878 --> 00:18:47,200
About five o’clock our procession of three cars reached the cemetery and

254
00:18:47,200 --> 00:18:53,080
stopped in a thick drizzle beside the gate— —first a motor hearse, horribly black

255
00:18:53,080 --> 00:18:57,939
and wet, then Mr. Gatz and the minister and I in the limousine,

256
00:18:57,939 --> 00:19:02,919
and a little later four or five servants and the postman from West Egg and Gatsby

257
00:19:02,919 --> 00:19:08,439
station wagon, all wet to the skin. As we started through the gate into the

258
00:19:08,439 --> 00:19:13,419
cemetery I heard a car stop and then the sound of someone splashing after us over

259
00:19:13,419 --> 00:19:15,218
the soggy ground.

260
00:19:15,218 --> 00:19:17,020
I looked around.

261
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:22,119
It was the man with the owl eyed glasses whom I had found marveling over Gatsby’s

262
00:19:22,119 --> 00:19:25,540
books in the library one night three months before.

263
00:19:25,540 --> 00:19:28,000
I’d never seen him since then.

264
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:33,040
I don’t know how he knew about the funeral, or even his name.

265
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:38,319
The rain poured down his thick glasses, and he took them off and wiped them to

266
00:19:38,319 --> 00:19:42,279
see the protecting canvas unrolled from Gatsby’s grave.

267
00:19:42,279 --> 00:19:48,939
I tried to think about Gatsby then for a moment, but he was already too far away,

268
00:19:48,939 --> 00:19:57,878
and I could only remember, without resentment, that Daisy hadn’t sent a message or a flower.

269
00:19:57,878 --> 00:20:05,200
Dimly I heard someone murmur “Blessed are the dead that the rain falls on,”

270
00:20:05,200 --> 00:20:13,240
and then the owl-eyed man said “Amen to that,” in a brave voice.

271
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:18,878
We straggled down quickly through the rain to the cars owl eyes spoke to me by

272
00:20:18,878 --> 00:20:23,979
the gate. “I couldn’t get to the house,” he remarked.

273
00:20:23,979 --> 00:20:29,439
“Neither could anybody else.” “Go on!” He started.

274
00:20:29,439 --> 00:20:32,919
“Why, my God! they used to go there by the hundreds.”

275
00:20:32,919 --> 00:20:37,900
He took off his glasses and wiped them again, outside and in.

276
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:42,159
“The poor son of a he said.

277
00:20:42,159 --> 00:20:47,439
One of my most vivid memories is of coming back West from prep school and

278
00:20:47,439 --> 00:20:49,957
later from college at Christmas time.

279
00:20:49,957 --> 00:20:53,979
Those who went farther than Chicago would gather in the old dim Union

280
00:20:53,979 --> 00:20:58,479
Station at six o’clock of a December evening, with a few Chicago friends,

281
00:20:58,479 --> 00:21:04,718
already caught up into their own holiday deities to bid them a hasty goodbye.

282
00:21:04,718 --> 00:21:09,637
I remember the fur coats of the girls returning from Miss this or that and the

283
00:21:09,637 --> 00:21:15,878
chatter of frozen breath and the hands waving overhead as we caught sight of old acquaintances,

284
00:21:15,878 --> 00:21:20,797
and the matchings of invitations: “Are you going to the Ordways’? the

285
00:21:20,797 --> 00:21:28,119
Hersheys the Schultzes’?” and the long green tickets clasped tight in our gloved hands.

286
00:21:28,119 --> 00:21:32,797
And the last murky yellow cars of the Chicago, Milwaukee and Saint Paul

287
00:21:32,797 --> 00:21:38,200
railroad looking cheerful as Christmas itself on the tracks beside the gate.

288
00:21:38,200 --> 00:21:44,200
When we pulled out into the winter night and the real snow hour snow, began to

289
00:21:44,200 --> 00:21:48,457
stretch out beside us and twinkle against the windows, and the dim lights

290
00:21:48,457 --> 00:21:56,200
of small Wisconsin stations moved by, a sharp wild brace came suddenly into the air.

291
00:21:56,200 --> 00:22:01,060
We drew in deep breaths of it as we walked back from dinner through the cold

292
00:22:01,060 --> 00:22:06,040
vestibules unnerably aware of our identity with this country for one

293
00:22:06,040 --> 00:22:10,779
strange hour, before we melted indistinguishably into it again.

294
00:22:10,779 --> 00:22:15,340
That’s my Middle West— —not the wheat or the prairies or the

295
00:22:15,340 --> 00:22:20,560
lost Swede towns, but the thrilling returning trains of my youth,

296
00:22:20,560 --> 00:22:24,520
and the street lamps and sleigh bells and the frosty dark

297
00:22:24,520 --> 00:22:29,319
and the shadows of holly wreaths thrown by lighted windows in the snow.

298
00:22:29,319 --> 00:22:35,259
I am part of that, a little solemn with the feel of those long winters,

299
00:22:35,259 --> 00:22:39,759
a little complacent from growing up in the Caraway house in a city where

300
00:22:39,759 --> 00:22:44,319
dwellings are still called through decades by a family’s name.

301
00:22:44,319 --> 00:22:48,099
I see now that this has been a story of the West,

302
00:22:48,099 --> 00:22:55,419
after all— —Tom and Gatsby, Daisy and Jordan and I, were all Westerners,

303
00:22:55,419 --> 00:23:02,200
and perhaps we possessed some deficiency in common which made us subtly unadaptable to

304
00:23:02,200 --> 00:23:03,759
Eastern life.

305
00:23:03,759 --> 00:23:09,218
Even when the East excited me most, even when I was most keenly aware of its

306
00:23:09,218 --> 00:23:14,619
superiority to the board sprawling, swollen towns beyond the Ohio, with their

307
00:23:14,619 --> 00:23:19,479
interminable inquisitions which spared only the children in the very old—

308
00:23:19,479 --> 00:23:24,700
—even then it had always for me a quality of distortion.

309
00:23:24,700 --> 00:23:30,637
West Egg, especially, still figures in my more fantastic dreams. I see it as a

310
00:23:30,637 --> 00:23:37,439
night scene by Al Greco: a hundred houses, at once conventional and grotesque,

311
00:23:37,439 --> 00:23:43,840
crouching under a sullen, overhanging sky and a lusterless moon.

312
00:23:43,840 --> 00:23:48,457
In the foreground four solemn men in dress suits or walking along the

313
00:23:48,457 --> 00:23:54,159
sidewalk with a stretcher on which lies a drunken woman in a white evening dress.

314
00:23:54,159 --> 00:23:59,979
Her hand, which dangles over the sides sparkles cold with jewels. Gravely the

315
00:23:59,979 --> 00:24:02,860
men turn into the house— —the wrong house.

316
00:24:02,860 --> 00:24:07,840
But no one knows the woman’s name, and no one cares.

317
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:12,759
After Gatsby’s death the East was haunted for me like that, distorted

318
00:24:12,759 --> 00:24:15,878
beyond my eyes’ power of correction.

319
00:24:15,878 --> 00:24:20,860
So when the blue smoke of brittle leaves was in the air and the wind blew the wet

320
00:24:20,860 --> 00:24:25,900
laundry stiff on the line I decided to come back home.

321
00:24:25,900 --> 00:24:31,180
There was one thing to be done before I left, an awkward, unpleasant thing that

322
00:24:31,180 --> 00:24:33,878
perhaps had better have been left alone.

323
00:24:33,878 --> 00:24:38,919
But I wanted to leave things in order and not just trust that obliging and

324
00:24:38,919 --> 00:24:42,520
indifferent sea to sweep my refuse away.

325
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:48,637
I saw Jordan Baker and talked over and around what had happened to us together,

326
00:24:48,637 --> 00:24:56,500
and what had happened afterward to me, and she lay perfectly still, listening, in a big chair.

327
00:24:56,500 --> 00:25:02,560
She was dressed to play golf, and I remember thinking she looked like a good illustration,

328
00:25:02,560 --> 00:25:08,439
her chin raised a little jauntly her hair the color of an autumn leaf, her

329
00:25:08,439 --> 00:25:13,060
face the same brown tint as the fingerless glove on her knee.

330
00:25:13,060 --> 00:25:18,700
When I had finished she told me without comment that she was engaged to another man.

331
00:25:18,700 --> 00:25:24,759
I doubted that, though there were several she could have married at a knot of her head,

332
00:25:24,759 --> 00:25:27,457
but I pretended to be surprised.

333
00:25:27,457 --> 00:25:31,297
For just a minute I wondered if I wasn’t making a mistake,

334
00:25:31,297 --> 00:25:36,279
then I thought it all over again quickly and got up to say goodbye.

335
00:25:36,279 --> 00:25:43,718
“Nevertheless you did throw me over,” said Jordan suddenly. “You threw me over on the telephone.

336
00:25:43,718 --> 00:25:48,700
I don’t give a damn about you now, but it was a new experience for me,

337
00:25:48,700 --> 00:25:54,400
and I felt a little dizzy for a while.” We shook hands.

338
00:25:54,400 --> 00:26:02,020
“Oh, and do you remember”— ”—she added—“ —“a conversation we had once about driving a car?”

339
00:26:02,020 --> 00:26:09,939
“Why— —not exactly.” “You said a bad driver was only safe until she met another bad driver?

340
00:26:09,939 --> 00:26:15,759
Well, I met another bad driver, didn’t die I mean it was careless of me to make

341
00:26:15,759 --> 00:26:21,400
such a wrong guess. I thought you were rather an honest, straightforward person.

342
00:26:21,400 --> 00:26:25,297
I thought it was your secret pride.” “I’m 30.

343
00:26:25,297 --> 00:26:26,740
I said.

344
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:33,580
“I’m five years too old to lie to myself and call it honor She didn’t answer.

345
00:26:33,580 --> 00:26:41,137
Angry, and half in love with her, and tremendously sorry, I turned away.

346
00:26:41,137 --> 00:26:45,457
One afternoon late in October I saw Tom Buchanan.

347
00:26:45,457 --> 00:26:50,200
He was walking ahead of me along Fifth Avenue in his alert, aggressive way,

348
00:26:50,200 --> 00:26:55,479
his hands out a little from his body as if to fight off interference,

349
00:26:55,479 --> 00:27:00,700
his head moving sharply here and there, adapting itself to his restless eyes.

350
00:27:00,700 --> 00:27:07,000
Just as I slowed up to avoid overtaking him he stopped and began frowning into

351
00:27:07,000 --> 00:27:09,279
the windows of a jewelry store.

352
00:27:09,279 --> 00:27:13,180
Suddenly he saw me and walked back, holding out his hand.

353
00:27:13,180 --> 00:27:17,020
“What’s the matter, Nick? Do you object to shaking hands with me?”

354
00:27:17,020 --> 00:27:21,218
“Yes. You know what I think of you.” “You’re crazy, Nick,”

355
00:27:21,218 --> 00:27:25,779
he said quickly. “Crazy as hell. I don’t know what’s the matter with you.”

356
00:27:25,779 --> 00:27:33,637
“Tom,” I inquired, “what did you say to Wilson that afternoon?” He stared at me without a word,

357
00:27:33,637 --> 00:27:38,740
and I knew I had guessed right about those missing hours.

358
00:27:38,740 --> 00:27:44,020
I started to turn away, but he took a step after me and grabbed my arm. “I told

359
00:27:44,020 --> 00:27:46,599
him the truth,” he said.

360
00:27:46,599 --> 00:27:50,439
“He came to the door we were getting ready to leave, and when I sent down word

361
00:27:50,439 --> 00:27:55,060
that we weren’t in he tried to force his way upstairs. He was crazy enough to kill

362
00:27:55,060 --> 00:27:57,759
me if I hadn’t told him who owns the car.

363
00:27:57,759 --> 00:28:03,159
His hand was on a revolver in his pocket every minute he was in the house—”

364
00:28:03,159 --> 00:28:08,797
He broke off defiantly. “What if I did tell him? That fellow had it coming to

365
00:28:08,797 --> 00:28:13,297
him. He threw dust into your eyes just like he did in Daisies

366
00:28:13,297 --> 00:28:18,279
but he was a tough one. He ran over Myrtle like he'd run over a dog and

367
00:28:18,279 --> 00:28:22,718
never even stopped his car.” There was nothing I could say,

368
00:28:22,718 --> 00:28:28,900
except the one unutterable fact that it wasn’t true.

369
00:28:28,900 --> 00:28:33,340
“And if you think I didn’t have my share of suffering— —look here, when I went to

370
00:28:33,340 --> 00:28:37,419
give up that flat and saw that damn box of dog biscuits sitting there on the sideboard,

371
00:28:37,419 --> 00:28:40,060
I sat down and cried like a baby.

372
00:28:40,060 --> 00:28:45,099
By God it was awful—” I couldn’t forgive him or like him,

373
00:28:45,099 --> 00:28:51,457
but I saw that what he had done was, to him, entirely justified.

374
00:28:51,457 --> 00:28:54,819
It was all very careless and confused.

375
00:28:54,819 --> 00:29:01,297
They were careless people, Tom and Daisy— —they smashed up things and creatures and

376
00:29:01,297 --> 00:29:06,040
then retreated back into their money and their vast carelessness,

377
00:29:06,040 --> 00:29:11,259
or whatever it was that kept them together, and let other people clean up the mess

378
00:29:11,259 --> 00:29:17,439
they had made… I shook hands with him; it seemed silly not to,

379
00:29:17,439 --> 00:29:21,520
for I felt suddenly as though I were talking to a child.

380
00:29:21,520 --> 00:29:26,200
Then he went into the jewelry store to buy a pearl necklace— —or perhaps only a

381
00:29:26,200 --> 00:29:32,919
pair of cuff buttons— —rid of my provincial squeamishness forever.

382
00:29:32,919 --> 00:29:38,740
Gatsby’s house was still empty when I left— —the grass on his lawn had grown as long

383
00:29:38,740 --> 00:29:40,419
as mine.

384
00:29:40,419 --> 00:29:44,740
One of the taxi drivers in the village never took a fare past the entrance gate

385
00:29:44,740 --> 00:29:50,740
without stopping for a minute and pointing inside; perhaps it was he who drove Daisy and

386
00:29:50,740 --> 00:29:54,099
Gatsby over to East Egg the night of the accident,

387
00:29:54,099 --> 00:29:56,259
and perhaps he had made a story about it

388
00:29:56,259 --> 00:29:59,439
all his own. I didn’t want to hear it and

389
00:29:59,439 --> 00:30:02,619
I avoided him when I got off the train.

390
00:30:02,619 --> 00:30:07,599
I spent my Saturday nights in New York because those gleaming, dazzling parties

391
00:30:07,599 --> 00:30:12,939
of his were with me so vividly that I could still hear the music and the laughter,

392
00:30:12,939 --> 00:30:19,659
faint and incessant, from his garden, and the car is going up and down his drive.

393
00:30:19,659 --> 00:30:26,797
One night I did hear a material car there, and saw its light stop at his front steps.

394
00:30:26,797 --> 00:30:29,080
But I didn’t investigate.

395
00:30:29,080 --> 00:30:34,540
Probably it was some final guest who had been away at the ends of the earth

396
00:30:34,540 --> 00:30:37,840
and didn’t know that the party was over.

397
00:30:37,840 --> 00:30:43,540
On the last night, with my trunk packed and my car sold to the grocer, I went

398
00:30:43,540 --> 00:30:49,240
over and looked at that huge incoherent failure of a house once more.

399
00:30:49,240 --> 00:30:54,279
On the white steps an obscene word, scrawled by some boy with a piece of

400
00:30:54,279 --> 00:31:01,599
brick, stood out clearly in the moonlight, and I erased it, drawing my shoe raspingly along the

401
00:31:01,599 --> 00:31:02,860
stone.

402
00:31:02,860 --> 00:31:07,840
Then I wandered down to the beach and sprawled out on the sand.

403
00:31:07,840 --> 00:31:13,000
Most of the big shore places were closed now and there were hardly any lights except

404
00:31:13,000 --> 00:31:17,259
the shadowy, moving glow of a ferry boat across the Sound.

405
00:31:17,259 --> 00:31:21,939
And as the moon rose higher the inessential houses began to melt away

406
00:31:21,939 --> 00:31:27,457
until gradually I became aware of the old I grew up again closer word once for

407
00:31:27,457 --> 00:31:33,040
Dutch sailors’ eyes— —a fresh, green breast of the new world.

408
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:38,500
Its vanished trees, the trees that had made way for Gatsby’s house, had once

409
00:31:38,500 --> 00:31:43,360
pandered in whispers to the last and greatest of all human dreams;

410
00:31:43,360 --> 00:31:48,939
for a transitory enchanted moment man must have held his breath in the

411
00:31:48,939 --> 00:31:56,919
presence of this continent, compelled into an aesthetic contemplation he neither understood nor

412
00:31:56,919 --> 00:32:04,119
desired, face to face for the last time in history with something commensurate to

413
00:32:04,119 --> 00:32:07,659
his capacity for wonder.

414
00:32:07,659 --> 00:32:12,637
And as I sat there brooding on the old, unknown world,

415
00:32:12,637 --> 00:32:17,259
I thought of Gatsby’s wonder when he first picked out the green light at the

416
00:32:17,259 --> 00:32:22,659
end of Daisy’s dock. He had come a long way to this blue lawn,

417
00:32:22,659 --> 00:32:28,599
and his dream must have seemed so close that he could hardly fail to grasp it.

418
00:32:28,599 --> 00:32:34,659
He did not know that it was already behind him, somewhere back in that vast obscurity

419
00:32:34,659 --> 00:32:41,020
beyond the city, where the dark fields of the republic rolled on under the night.

420
00:32:41,020 --> 00:32:48,819
Gatsby believed in the green light, the orgastic future that year by year recedes before us.

421
00:32:48,819 --> 00:32:56,020
It eluded us then, but that’s no matter— —tomorrow we will run faster,

422
00:32:56,020 --> 00:33:01,740
stretch out our arms farther And one fine morning—

423
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:09,457
So we beat on, boats against the current born back ceaselessly
